|~ `::builtin`, the descriptor of a primitive, and what is declared
   under it: its constructor and its refusal, and the refusals of the
   runtime no reader reaches by a query, the table of primitives, the
   declarations of the refusals, a value of no kind a host handed and the
   sources of the root.
   The rules it holds: [QSHST#source], [QSERR#site], [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:builtin
  |~~ A builtin marks a verb a host answers by a primitive: the recipe of
      a function whose code the host hands, `::builtin{}`, where a verb
      written in steps holds a quote, into which the root writes the
      address it takes the primitive under, `:of`:
      `::number/add | binding | value | get /1` answers
      `::builtin{:of ::number/add}`. A kind of the core takes one,
      `| impl ::builtin{}`, as its constructor, the one its host hands,
      which attaches the tag to a payload or refuses it.

      It is a map under its tag and runs nothing by itself: a name that
      holds one reads it as data. A call of the verb runs the primitive
      its host handed, whose answer is a value of the language. A call of
      a function or a kind read back from its print runs it too, where the
      root holds one equal to it at the address its `:of` names.
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts)
  ~~|
  ::kind[~(accepts
        |~~ A map, the descriptor, its fields flat under the tag, `:of` the
            address the root took its primitive under.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::builtin` holds its fields as a map, `::builtin{}` the recipe
            a host's verb ends with and the constructor a kind of the core
            takes, `impl ::builtin{}`; a payload of another kind is refused,
            a map under another tag among them, and the descriptor is
            written `::builtin{}`.
        ~~|
    | throws :AddressRegisteredTwiceError
        |~~ Two modules of the runtime registered a primitive under one
            address, a defect of the runtime found as it loads. Each
            primitive is registered under the address of the verb or the
            kind it serves, one to an address, `number/add`.
        ~~|
    | throws :RefusalDeclaredTwiceError
        |~~ Two sites of the runtime declared one refusal under one
            address, a defect of the runtime found as it loads: the
            address is the tag the refusal answers by and the name of its
            page. Each site declares its
            refusal under the address of what refused, the verb or the
            input, one site to an address.
        ~~|
    | throws :RefusalFactNotDeclaredError
        |~~ A refusal of the runtime carried a fact the kind of its refusal
            does not wire, a defect of the runtime: the facts a refusal
            holds are the parts its kind wires. The fact is wired in the
            chain of the refusal, `throws` and its `wires`, a name, its page
            and the kinds it holds, `::path/IndexOutOfBoundsError/length`
            holding `::number`.
        ~~|
    | throws :AddressKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A module of the runtime registered a primitive under an address
            that is no string, a defect of the runtime found as it loads,
            `:found` naming the address as the runtime holds it. The
            address is the text of the verb's address, `number/add`.
        ~~|
    | throws :PrimitiveNotHandedError
        |~~ A name is `::builtin{}`, or a kind ends in one, and the host
            handed no primitive for it beside its source; `:origin` names
            the source and `:name` the name, and the root is refused as it
            is declared. The host hands a primitive for the name beside the
            source, or writes the name in steps, a quote, which runs
            without one.
        ~~|
    | throws :TableSealedError
        |~~ The table of the language's primitives is sealed when the
            bootstrap completes, and one registered after it is refused, a
            defect of the runtime: a module of the runtime registers its
            primitives as it loads, and a host hands its own beside its
            sources, never to the table.
        ~~|
    | throws :ForeignValueError
        |~~ A host handed the core a value of none of the kinds of the
            language where the core takes it, at any depth: as the subject
            of a cell, or as what a name holds in the scope it hands a
            session or a query. Such a value is a function, an object of
            the runtime, a map whose key is no string, a failure whose facts
            are no map, a value under a tag that is no tag, a vector or a map
            that holds itself, or a number past the finite doubles; a value
            the runtime leaves undefined reads as null. It is a defect of the
            host, raised past the fail track. `:found` names the value as
            the runtime holds it, its type and its name or its maker,
            `"function hello"`, or what makes a vector or a map of no kind.
            The printer and the codec of JSON raise it too for a value of no
            kind a host hands a cell.
            The answer of a primitive a host hands beside its source is held
            to the kinds at its call and refused as `::call/HostFailureError`,
            and a host builds a value of the language from the data it holds
            parsed by `fromPlain`.
        ~~|
    | throws :BootstrapSourceMissingError
        |~~ A source of the language resolved to nothing, so the root
            lacks what it declares; `:origin` names the source. The host
            maps the source where its package says the sources of the
            language stand, or hands the runtime a locator of its own.
        ~~|
    | throws :RootSourceNotDeclaredError
        |~~ A source of the root answered an error, or declared again a
            name the root holds, so its declarations stay out of the root;
            `:origin` names the source and `:tagName` the refusal, for a
            name declared again `::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError` with `:name`
            the name. The source is repaired by the page of that refusal,
            and a name declared again takes a name of its own, the root's
            left as they are.
        ~~|
    | throws :RootValueNotWordError
        |~~ A source of the root declared a value under a bare name, which
            serves the query of its own text and reaches no reader, so the
            source is refused and its declarations stay out of the root;
            `:origin` names the source and `:name` the name. A source of the
            root declares words, a kind, a verb under a label and a node
            under a noun, data under an address among them,
            `:Box/limit 10;`.
        ~~|
    | throws :PrimitiveNamesNoBuiltinError
        |~~ A host handed a primitive beside a source of the root for a
            name the source declares neither as a `::builtin{}` under a
            label nor as a kind ending in one, or for a name that holds the
            `::builtin{}` of another name; `:origin` names the source,
            `:name` the name and `:sharedWith` that other name. A verb
            written in steps runs them, a `::builtin{}` written among them
            the value it is. A bare name reads its value. A name that holds
            the value of another name runs the primitive of that name,
            while a function of its own over the same `::builtin{}` runs
            nothing. So a host declares its primitive as the recipe of a
            verb under the label of its subject,
            `:any/fetch (::fn | returns |~~ The text. ~~| ::string | impl ::builtin{});`.
            A `::builtin{}` the host hands nothing for is refused with
            `::builtin/PrimitiveNotHandedError`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :origin |~~ The source the primitive was handed beside. ~~| ::keyword
          | wires :name |~~ The name handed a primitive. ~~| ::keyword
          | wires :sharedWith |~~ The other name whose `::builtin{}` the name holds. ~~| ::keyword)
    | throws :SourceLoadError
        |~~ A source of the language was found where its package maps it,
            and could not be read; `:origin` names the source,
            `:sourceLocation` where it was looked for and `:cause` the
            host's account of the failure. A host that ships the sources of
            the language with it hands the runtime a locator of them, which
            reads each where it keeps them.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :origin |~~ The source that could not be read. ~~| ::keyword
          | wires :sourceLocation |~~ Where the platform looked for it. ~~| ::string
          | wires :cause |~~ The host's account of the failure. ~~|)
    | proves :isAValueOfItsOwn
        |~~ An empty descriptor is a value like any other, which prints and
            reads back as itself.
        ~~|
        ~(::builtin{} | eq (print | parse | answer))
    | proves :endsAPrimitive
        |~~ The recipe of a verb a host answers by a primitive is the
            descriptor of its address.
        ~~|
        ~(::number/add | binding | value | get /1 | eq ::builtin{:of ::number/add})
    | proves :readsBackAsItsPrimitive
        |~~ A function of a host read back from its print runs its primitive. ~~|
        ~(:f (::number/add | binding | value | print | parse | answer); 5 | apply f [1] | eq 6)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::builtin"hello" !| get [tag (found | tag)] | eq [::builtin/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::builtin[1 2 3] !| get [tag (found | tag)] | eq [::builtin/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::vec]))
    ::builtin{}];
